> In 1892, in Chicago, Mamah Borthwick  Cheney (née Cassatt) had just given  birth to  her second   child when a new family moved into town: Wright and his pregnant wife Kitty Lloyd-Jones. At twenty-five years old, Mamah was twelve years older  than Frank's thirteen-year-old  first son John. She invited both families to an evening social  and they became friends at  once.  That summer, everyone  went on vacation  together at the lake near Waukegan; during  that  trip, Wright began constructing a studio/bachelors'  pad for himself while Mamah continued to care  for a sickly Katie. When he finished building it a year  later, Wright offered it as  a gift to be  used by  any male  member of the household—a  gesture which left Mamah indignant toward   him.^{[3]} Two  summers after that, Wright  completed  work on his  home designed for  Mamah's husband  Edwin Cheney, affectionately nicknamed Taliesin after  an ancient Welsh bard  who inspired much of his work;   but   its construction  took longer because it was being built out of bricks instead  of the timber found in the rest of their  homes. By  this time in late December 1895, Wright  and Mamah  were having a heated affair, and he was also getting to know her  daughter Marian,  whom she brought over whenever school let  out for vacations.^{[4]} While on another vacation with  Mrs. Johnson  and Mrs. Lloyd Thomas, Wright would  have sex with Mamah under an open sky outside Taliesin without fear. One night thereafter,  Mamah said goodnight   to  everyone except Wright.^{[3]}^{[5]}
>
